rs are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07640a37-cc54-5ef1-91c5-4812836d51a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476874 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}$ is a surd because $\\frac{1}{6}$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}=0.408\\,248\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{9\\cdot 9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $100$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49},\\,\\sqrt{28},\\,\\sqrt{64}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6be9ff93-7c80-5a18-be7e-bd70edb8b02a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476908 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{49}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $49$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{}$) and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49}=\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}=7\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ is a surd because $28$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{28}=5.291\\,502\\,622\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{64}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{64}=\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10},\\,\\sqrt[3]{100},\\,\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7293d832-30bd-529a-a59d-7e39970dea7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476912 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{},$ cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{},$ $\\sqrt[5]{},…,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ is a surd because $10$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{10}=2.154\\,434\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ is a surd because $100$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{100}=4.641\\,588\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1\\,000$ is a perfect cube, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{})$ and obtain a whole number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & =\\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{42}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93bd4c15-92a4-568f-aaec-3b88b27585e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476924 #edb/ref 1074 "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{42}=6.480\\,740\\,698\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round the value to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{42}≈6.481.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a01f34bc-501b-51fb-84a4-4db1dd9276f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476929 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $36$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot 6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $9$ and $100$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}$ is a surd because $\\frac{11}{16}$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}=0.829\\,156\\,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{144},\\,\\sqrt{25},\\,\\sqrt{74}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aba568b7-e4b5-5a07-85ab-7669ffe98d64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476937 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{144}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since  $144$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{144}=\\sqrt{{12}^{2}}=12\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{25}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $25$  is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{25}=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}}=5\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{74}$ is a surd because $74$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{74}=8.602\\text{ }325\\,...$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{74}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{21}$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?\n\n$\\sqrt{21}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c21e2314-9d15-5091-bdd2-228be712839c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476958 #edb/ref 1074 "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21}=4.582\\,575\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round the value to $1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21}≈4.6\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5},\\,\\sqrt[3]{50},\\,\\sqrt[3]{125}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e15be35a-fdf2-54f2-a053-e59892bfa1cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476968 #edb/ref 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{},$ cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{},$ $\\sqrt[5]{},…,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is a surd because $5$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{5}=1.709\\,975\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is a surd because $50$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{50}=3.684\\,031\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{125}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $125$ is a perfect cube, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{})$ and obtain a whole number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{125} & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
